#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/id #uuid "a3234c09-08eb-56f2-8a4c-d6430d38c356" :question/math-academy-id "e-275" :question/problem "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/620/Source/Images/038c730db46c27977ecaf5b36ce200bc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm}$, its radius is\n\n$$\nr =6\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d r}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2) \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\pi r^2, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)= 4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$.\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "3c4f9912-4b08-5e11-8ef6-4e4ee35765c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-276" :question/problem "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p$, in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5)$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(b) &= \\frac{d}{db}(5e^{-0.2b}) \\\\\n&= 5e^{-0.2b} \\cdot (-0.2) \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(5) &= -e^{-0.2(5)} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{e}\n$$\n\ndollars per pound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f2eda994-e00a-5e9b-a26d-720d01e6fa07" :question/math-academy-id "e-6174" :question/problem "A particle moves in a straight line. After $t$ seconds the particle is $\\, f(t)=2t^3 - 5t + 9\\,$ feet away from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position as a function of $t$?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is equal to $f'(t)$.\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 5t + 9) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t$ seconds, the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is $6t^2 - 5$ feet per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd2b21f-a739-5a2c-8947-9ffb1b500757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076858} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3bf12f-57b3-53a2-931e-981a627ea615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076859} {:answer/id #uuid "e6965292-1b93-5b27-bc2a-5045c54beefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076860} {:answer/id #uuid "356f9d42-a444-5e88-ade0-fa7121a66ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10b^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076861} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1baaff-d2e6-57b8-91d3-b512f6cf233c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6965292-1b93-5b27-bc2a-5045c54beefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076860} :answer-field/id #uuid "882f7b55-1931-5130-b0d2-4f536cda651a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076857}] :question/id #uuid "6f5c7d68-86e4-553b-96a2-1701e7dfe5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-48725" :question/problem "The area of the rectangle is increasing. The base of the rectangle is $b$, and its height is $10$ times its base. Find the rate of change of the area of a rectangle with respect to its base."}]}
